WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:04.340
Speaker 1: Let's speak to some of these great
people, members and nonmembers alike.

00:00:04.340 --> 00:00:11.160
We take calls on Fridays via Discord at David
Pakman dot com slash discord.

00:00:11.160 --> 00:00:13.420
Let's go next to Alex from Baltimore, Alex.

00:00:13.420 --> 00:00:14.420
Go ahead.

00:00:14.420 --> 00:00:16.560
Speaker 2: Hey, David, can you hear me?

00:00:16.560 --> 00:00:17.730
Yes, I can.

00:00:17.730 --> 00:00:19.840
It's cool.

00:00:19.840 --> 00:00:24.140
I got a question about it.

00:00:24.140 --> 00:00:28.039
I got a question about a publicly funded elections.

00:00:28.039 --> 00:00:29.359
Sure.

00:00:29.359 --> 00:00:30.689
So.

00:00:30.689 --> 00:00:38.480
I know it's a long shot in the United States,
but so hypothetically, if one were to happen.

00:00:38.480 --> 00:00:45.329
How does the money get doled out because I
know, you know, if it's a governor or president,

00:00:45.329 --> 00:00:50.340
technically there's like eight independents
and a bunch of other random parties no one's

00:00:50.340 --> 00:00:51.340
heard of.

00:00:51.340 --> 00:00:55.190
So do they get a share of the money or is
it do they have to meet a certain qualification?

00:00:55.190 --> 00:00:57.300
Speaker 1: This is a great question.

00:00:57.300 --> 00:00:58.300
This is a great question.

00:00:58.300 --> 00:01:02.930
So listen, there's no one way to set this
up, but you could you could establish.

00:01:02.930 --> 00:01:04.689
So what do we mean by public funding of elections?

00:01:04.689 --> 00:01:09.900
The idea is to limit the amount of money that
can go into an election, and it and I have

00:01:09.900 --> 00:01:12.069
to say it's so loud where you are.

00:01:12.069 --> 00:01:16.670
Alex, would it be OK if maybe I mute you to
answer, because this is a very important issue?

00:01:16.670 --> 00:01:19.569
Speaker 2: Hang on, can you hear me better
right now?

00:01:19.569 --> 00:01:20.740
Speaker 1: Yeah, I can hear you fine.

00:01:20.740 --> 00:01:24.180
It's just there's so much background noise,
so the way we would set it up would be.

00:01:24.180 --> 00:01:31.200
We might say, OK, once you meet a certain
signature threshold, then you qualify and

00:01:31.200 --> 00:01:32.369
it can't be like 300 signatures, right?

00:01:32.369 --> 00:01:34.920
It really has to be significant.

00:01:34.920 --> 00:01:36.810
So you don't have 100 people.

00:01:36.810 --> 00:01:43.070
Then you get X amount of money, you get x
amount of local television time and whatever.

00:01:43.070 --> 00:01:47.450
You could do it on a polling basis, you have
to be polling at least X percent.

00:01:47.450 --> 00:01:51.840
Now some people will say, Well, the problem
is you need money to get the polling and it

00:01:51.840 --> 00:01:52.840
OK.

00:01:52.840 --> 00:01:54.709
But so that's that's another way that it could
be looked at.

00:01:54.709 --> 00:02:00.340
You also might have different thresholds,
depending on the population of the state or

00:02:00.340 --> 00:02:02.170
the race that you're you're running.

00:02:02.170 --> 00:02:06.560
So you wouldn't necessarily expect, you know,
a Senate candidate and a congressional candidate

00:02:06.560 --> 00:02:11.659
to have the same thresholds in terms of signatures,
at least because one is drawing from a much

00:02:11.659 --> 00:02:13.040
larger population base.

00:02:13.040 --> 00:02:16.069
So there's not one answer to the question.

00:02:16.069 --> 00:02:19.550
But it's a really important question that
has to be crystal clear when you put these

00:02:19.550 --> 00:02:20.680
systems in place.

00:02:20.680 --> 00:02:22.390
Speaker 2: OK, cool.

00:02:22.390 --> 00:02:24.200
Yeah, I figured that was the end.

00:02:24.200 --> 00:02:28.459
I also didn't know, is there any examples
of other places that do it?

00:02:28.459 --> 00:02:32.379
Speaker 1: I don't have any offhand, to be
perfectly frank.

00:02:32.379 --> 00:02:33.379
Speaker 2: Hmm, interesting.

00:02:33.379 --> 00:02:34.379
Yeah.

00:02:34.379 --> 00:02:35.379
Let me
Speaker 1: research that.

00:02:35.379 --> 00:02:37.469
